          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Michelangelo</strong><br>\nMichelangelo Buonarroti was born on March 6, 1475, in Caprese, Italy. He was raised in Florence, a cultural hub during the Renaissance. Michelangelo's mother died when he was six, after which he lived with a stonecutter's family, beginning his lifelong connection with marble. His artistic talent was evident early on, and he was sent to study under the prominent painter Domenico Ghirlandaio.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Artistic Training</strong><br>\nBy the age of 13, Michelangelo had started an apprenticeship with Ghirlandaio. His talent outshone his peers, leading to a recommendation to the ruler of Florence, Lorenzo de’ Medici. Michelangelo studied classical sculpture in the Medici gardens, absorbing the skills that would define his work. He was exposed to the city's intellectuals and artists, which broadened his perspective on art and culture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influences and Themes</strong><br>\nMichelangelo's work was heavily influenced by classical antiquity and the human form. He believed the body was a vessel for divine expression, often depicting muscular detail and emotional intensity. He was also inspired by religious themes, as seen in his many sculptures and paintings, including the David, the Pieta, and the Sistine Chapel ceiling.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
