          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Statue of David</strong><br>\nThe Statue of David, completed by Michelangelo in 1504, is one of his most famous works. Carved from a single block of marble, it represents the biblical hero David and is renowned for its detailed portrayal of human anatomy. This masterpiece is often considered a symbol of Renaissance art and Florentine pride.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Sistine Chapel Ceiling</strong><br>\nFrom 1508 to 1512, Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling, commissioned by Pope Julius II. This monumental work includes over 300 figures and illustrates scenes from the Book of Genesis, including the iconic creation of Adam. It showcases Michelangelo's mastery of human anatomy and composition in painting.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Last Judgment</strong><br>\nLater, Michelangelo returned to the Sistine Chapel to paint \"The Last Judgment\" on the altar wall, which he completed in 1541. This dramatic fresco depicts the second coming of Christ and the final judgment of souls. It was a shift from his earlier work, with a darker tone reflecting his own religious contemplation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
