          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Architectural Works</strong><br>\nMichelangelo was also a prominent architect. He designed the Laurentian Library and its famous staircase in Florence, as well as the dome of St. Peter's Basilica in Rome. His architectural designs blended his understanding of space with his sculptor's eye for form and detail.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Poetry and Personal Life</strong><br>\nIn addition to visual arts, Michelangelo was a prolific poet. He wrote hundreds of sonnets and madrigals, often reflecting on love, beauty, and spirituality. His personal life was marked by a deep religious faith, bouts of melancholy, and a dedication to his work, despite physical discomfort and old age.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nMichelangelo continued to work until his death on February 18, 1564. He left behind a legacy that has made him one of the most admired artists in history. His works are considered masterpieces of Renaissance art, and his techniques continue to influence artists today. Michelangelo's life and creations stand as testaments to the human spirit's capacity for extraordinary achievement.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
